Write a self-contained Python 3 command-line script that wraps raw HTML body
content (such as a CodePen-style snippet) into a complete standalone HTML5
document. **Purpose** The script reads an HTML fragment from a file, wraps it in
a full HTML5 page skeleton, and writes the result to an output file.
It is intended to run under Termux on Android, so the shebang should point to
the Termux Python path (`/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`).
**Behavior and structure to reproduce** 1.
Start the file with the Termux-style shebang and `from __future__ import
annotations`.
Import `sys` and `pathlib.Path`. 2.
Provide a pure function that takes the HTML body fragment, an optional `title`
(default `"Document"`), and an optional `charset` (default `"UTF-8"`).
It returns a complete HTML5 document string with: - a `<!DOCTYPE html>`
declaration and `<html lang="en">`; - a `<head>` containing a UTF-8 (or the
given) meta charset, a viewport meta tag (`width=device-width,
initial-scale=1.0`), the title, and a stylesheet link to `style.css` (relative
href); - a `<body>` containing the fragment verbatim, followed by a script tag
loading `script.js` (relative src), then closing tags.
Build the string with an f-string, keeping the output format exact (newlines and
indentation of four spaces as shown in a conventional HTML template). 3.
Provide a `process_file` function with parameters `input_file`,
`output_file=None`, and `title=None`: - Read `input_file` using UTF-8 encoding.
On `FileNotFoundError`, print an error message naming the file and return
`False`.
On any other exception, print an error including the exception text and return
`False`. - If `title` is `None`, derive it from the input file's stem: replace
hyphens with spaces and apply title-case (e.g., `my-page.html` becomes `My
Page`), using `pathlib.Path`. - Call the wrapping function with the content and
title. - If `output_file` is `None`, overwrite the input file in place. - Write
the result to the output file in UTF-8.
On success, print a check-marked line stating the input was successfully
converted, and a second check-marked line stating where it was saved, then
return `True`.
On a write exception, print an error message with the exception text and return
`False` (the exact wording of the write-failure branch was cut off in the
source, so use a sensible message beginning with "Error"). 4.
Include a command-line entry point guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":` that
uses `sys.argv`: - Accept an input file path as the first argument, an optional
output file path as the second, and an optional title as the third (or
equivalent sensible positional handling). - If no input argument is given, print
a usage message showing how to invoke the script and exit with a nonzero status.
- Call `process_file` with the parsed arguments and exit with status 0 on
success or 1 on failure (based on the boolean return value). **Edge cases and
notes** - Missing input file must be handled gracefully without a traceback. -
Non-UTF-8 input will be caught by the generic exception handler when reading. -
Output file defaults to overwriting the input file; the agent should make this
explicit in usage text. - Titles derived from filenames should handle multiple
hyphens and mixed case consistently. - Do not add third-party dependencies; use
only the standard library. - Keep the code minimal, readable, and consistent
with typical Python style (PEP 8, docstrings optional but concise).
**Deliverable** A single runnable Python script file implementing all of the
above, with no external dependencies. ---
